WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.420
Secure message queue operation starts with inventory.

2
00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:21.800
List producers, brokers, exchanges, queues, topics, subscriptions, consumers, worker pools, retry paths, dead-letter queues, schedulers, downstream services, credentials, logs, metrics, and owners.

3
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:32.300
Map trust boundaries and business actions so reviewers can see who creates messages, what data they contain, who can inspect them, who can consume them, and what actions they trigger.

4
00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:38.260
Apply least privilege to producers, consumers, administrators, and worker identities.

5
00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:44.840
Protect broker credentials, connection strings, certificates, and service tokens as secrets.

6
00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:51.260
Define message schemas, versions, required fields, allowed types, and size limits.

7
00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:54.160
Validate payloads at every consumer boundary.

8
00:00:54.260 --> 00:01:05.300
Do not trust user IDs, tenant IDs, object keys, roles, URLs, amounts, or workflow state just because the message arrived through a broker.

9
00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:09.100
Keep secrets and unnecessary sensitive data out of messages.

10
00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:17.440
Design consumers for idempotency, replay protection, duplicate processing, ordering assumptions, and safe retries.

11
00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:28.580
Govern dead-letter queues, replay tools, logs, traces, support dashboards, and investigation workflows because they may store sensitive payloads or trigger important actions.

12
00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:36.600
Harden worker runtimes with narrow permissions, updated packages, controlled concurrency, graceful shutdown, and useful monitoring.

13
00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:57.280
A practical first-week plan is straightforward: inventory queue flows, document schemas, review broker permissions, remove secrets from payloads, add idempotency to high-impact workers, review dead-letter access, check retry settings, add safe logs and metrics, and define owners for critical queues.

14
00:01:57.380 --> 00:02:09.540
Then build those checks into pull requests, CI/CD, testing, monitoring, runbooks, and incident response so queue security stays current as the system evolves.
